  {
    "sha": "7ab1d7689123cfc754d9592ac27bc62f2a11181f",
    "node_id": "C_kwDOVoU8JdoAKDdhYjFkNzY4OTEyM2NmYzc1NGQ5NTkyYWMyN2JjNjJmMmExMTE4MWY",
    "commit": {
      "author": {
        "name": "Mike Kinney",
        "email": "mike.kinney@gmail.com",
        "date": "2026-11-12T03:16:09Z"
      },
      "committer": {
        "name": "Mike Kinney",
        "email": "mike.kinney@gmail.com",
        "date": "2026-11-12T03:16:09Z"
      },
      "message": "fix(post): the Fanuc lathe seats an off-seat face cycle by the hop law\n\nA face cycle (G83/G84/G85) read with the tool off its seat (on centre\nat R) was reached by one G00 X0 Z<R> - a diagonal X+Z rapid that, from\nbelow R and off centre, runs toward the face. fanuc-lathe.cps now takes\n#994's cycleSeat: Z out to R first when below it, then X onto the\ncentre, then down to R when above it. +Z is away from the face (the\npost's own Z law; it checks the record's bottom is below R). A cycle it\ncannot seat - its section's first motion, R not above its bottom, or a\nno-R record off the axis - is refused by name. tormach-lathe writes no\npositioning block of its own and is unchanged; its plain moves are the\nstream's, pinned.\n\nThe #1011 read-back checker also reads the seat now. Two synthetic\nlathe_post_refusals fixtures that read a cycle as the section's first\nmotion gain the approach rapid the generator's streams carry.\n\nFixes #1026\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>",
